WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:01.900
So I have to ask.

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:05.500
So you are pro-industry, but you’re also pro, it sounds like, environment.

3
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.500
You want conservation and you want . . .

4
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:08.440
Yeah.

5
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.600
How do you reconcile that when it sometimes doesn’t . . .?

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.460
Well, it does.

7
00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:15.500
It really does.

8
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:19.760
I mean we have the agencies that watch the industries.

9
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:28.560
I was part of the environmental group for five years, so I have first-hand knowledge of going on inspections with the DEQ [Department of Environmental Quality].

10
00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:37.080
I have first-hand knowledge of the EPA [Environmental Protection Agency] being in the plant, and those guys take their job very serious and they watch out.

11
00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:41.560
And that was in 1999.

12
00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:52.500
The industries, like everything else out there, whether it’s a computer or any . . . from the iPhones,

13
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:01.800
have exponentially grown greatly in their ability to be more efficient.

14
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:08.040
The industries, they don’t . . . They try not to have any upsets,

15
00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:12.560
and try to not to hurt our environment, and they have checks and balances in there.

16
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:25.380
I was a part of the era that in the ‘70s wasn’t near as restricted as it is now.

17
00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:33.040
But we wasn’t ever hurting the environment, we . . . But they’re very strict and they watch it.

18
00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.740
And they’re very . . . We’ve got really good agencies that watch our environment.

19
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:40.080
So I have a lot of faith in what those guys doing.

20
00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:45.180
I mean I don’t feel like our plants are here . . .

21
00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:52.920
Is there a bit of a trade off with the lights and the noise and the traffic and things like this?

22
00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.100
Absolutely.

23
00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:55.800
But look at the good jobs we have.

24
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:59.300
Look at . . . As I talked earlier about the 401Ks, and insurance,

25
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:05.780
and the great lives you can have, and the vacations you can take,

26
00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:13.840
and the homecoming gowns you can buy for your kids, and afford things because you have a good job in the industry.

27
00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:15.840
So, yeah.

28
00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.060
And I am an outdoorsman, and they put a lot back.

29
00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.200
They put a lot back to the outdoors.

30
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:26.540
Or as an auctioneer I often see whether it’s the industry itself,

31
00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:39.520
or a contractor of the industry spending money with Delta Waterfowl, Ducks Unlimited, Whitetail Deer, the different auctions that I get to do.

32
00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:43.960
They put a lot back into the community and . . .

33
00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:47.540
How about any accidental spills?

34
00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:53.760
Like I know . . . I don’t know if it was Conoco, but Condea Vista in the . . . Mossville.

35
00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:54.700
I don’t know if you remember.

36
00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:55.680
Yeah the EDC [ethylene dichloride] spill?

37
00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:56.260
[Agrees].

38
00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:57.420
Yeah.

39
00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:58.520
Do you remember?

40
00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:00.360
Yeah I was right in the middle of it.

41
00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.140
I know all about it.

42
00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:06.100
And I know that there’s been remediation of that.

43
00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:10.940
There was a . . . They’d put all kind of wells in.

44
00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:13.960
That we used to check the sands in those wells.

45
00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:20.200
Check to see if there was any contamination and then different levels whether it was twenty foot,

46
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:26.400
eighty foot, hundred foot . . . but there has been mistakes made.

47
00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:34.160
I mean my gosh, we’re all capable of not doing a hundred percent all the time.

48
00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.180
But they’ve done a lot of work to remediate all that.

49
00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:46.900
And they bought out a lot of people in that Bel Air area that they thought was affected.

50
00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:51.520
So they’ve done the right thing in my mind to make things better.

51
00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:57.240
And anytime you can have what essentially started out as a nine billion dollar project,

52
00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:03.060
and now eleven billion dollar project, right here in my police jury district, in my hometown,

53
00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:16.020
that hopefully my kids and grandkids will have opportunity to work at and stay here in southwest Louisiana and not have to move off because they don’t have opportunities here.

54
00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:23.460
And that’s one thing that I guess as an elected official working with these industries is give the local people first shot.

55
00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:25.940
And they have been pretty good about that.
